\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Warehouse Scenario Schematic.} The warehouse setting: 10 robots with heterogeneous capabilities (strength~⚡, sensing~📷, mobility~🚗) and 5 tasks with varying requirements. Classical schedulers struggle with the combinatorial explosion. Neuromorphic approaches exploit the structure of the problem. }}{23}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Conflict Graph --- 7-Node Worked Example.} Each node represents a feasible (coalition, task) pair with size proportional to utility weight. Red edges indicate robot conflicts; blue edges indicate task conflicts. The maximum weight independent set is $\{v_0, v_1\}$ (shown highlighted), corresponding to allocation $(r_1 \to t_2, r_3 \to t_1)$ with total utility $5.0 + 6.0 = 11.0$. }}{24}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {OIM Phase Trajectories --- Worked Example.} Each colored line shows one oscillator's phase over time. Initially, phases are random. Coupling drives them toward either 0 or $\pi $ (binarized states). By $t \approx 100$ ms, phases have stabilized, revealing the solution $s = [+1, +1, -1, -1, -1]^T$, corresponding to selecting nodes $v_0, v_1$. }}{29}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Scalability Plot --- Graph Size vs. Pruning Strategy.} Horizontal axis: number of robots $N$. Vertical axis: $|V|$ (conflict graph nodes). Blue line: no pruning (exponential growth). Green line: with coalition bounding $k_{\max } = 2$ (quadratic). Orange line: with coalition bounding + spatial pruning (near-linear in realistic geometries). The shaded region marks OIM hardware feasibility window (100--2000 nodes for current hardware). }}{30}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Coalition Size vs. Hardware Nodes: Scalability Frontier.} Shows how the number of conflict graph nodes scales with the number of robots for different maximum coalition sizes $k = 1, 2, 3, 4$. Horizontal dashed lines indicate hardware feasibility regions: 100-node OIM (red), 2000-node OIM (orange), 10k-node FeFET (green). The feasibility window shows realistic problem sizes that fit current hardware. }}{30}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Hybrid Pipeline Block Diagram.} Pre-processing (classical): enumerate feasible coalitions, build conflict graph, compute utilities. OIM solve: physical system minimizes Hamiltonian, returns binary phases. Post-processing: convert phases to allocation, check feasibility, repair any constraint violations via greedy heuristics. }}{32}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces \textbf {OIM Solver Performance: Real-Time Feasibility.} Mean OIM solve time (blue line with error bars) vs. conflict graph size $|V|$. Orange and red dashed lines indicate real-time deadlines: 20 ms (50 Hz) and 15 ms (66 Hz) respectively. The figure demonstrates that OIM solutions scale gracefully and remain within robotics real-time budgets even for graphs with 300+ nodes, outperforming classical solvers in this regime. }}{32}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Anti-ferromagnetic Coupling Intuition.} (a) Two oscillators with positive coupling: prefer same phase (both at 0 or both at $\pi $). (b) Two oscillators with negative (anti-ferromagnetic) coupling: prefer opposite phases (one at 0, one at $\pi $). This is the conflict constraint: ``at most one can be selected.'' }}{34}{figure.caption.24}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Linearization Accuracy: Deviation vs. Error.} Shows how linearization error grows with joint angle deviation from equilibrium. Green shaded region indicates the valid linearization zone ($|\Delta \theta | < 10°$ with error $< 5\%$). Red vertical line marks the 10° deviation limit beyond which nonlinear effects dominate. This plot validates our choice of linear MPC for near-equilibrium trajectory tracking in robotics applications. }}{38}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {MPC Horizon Trade-off: Accuracy vs. Computation.} Left y-axis (blue): PIPG iterations required to converge as a function of prediction horizon $N$. Right y-axis (orange): normalized solution quality. The figure shows that longer horizons $(N > 10)$ provide diminishing returns in quality while significantly increasing computational cost. $N=5$ offers a good balance for real-time robotics applications with tight latency constraints. }}{38}{figure.caption.27}%
